# Localization and Internationalization

Defines how user-facing text, formatting, and accessibility labels are handled.

## Mandatory rules

- Must not hard-code user-facing text in widgets, controllers, validators, or error views.
- Must use Flutter generated localization from `lib/l10n`; enable `flutter: generate: true` in `pubspec.yaml`; keep `l10n.yaml` at project root.
- English is the initial locale; every future locale must have its own ARB file.
- Must use locale-aware formatting for dates, times, numbers, currency, and plurals.
- Keys must be descriptive and stable; copy concise and clinically appropriate.
- Must include accessibility labels and error messages in localization files.
- Must not concatenate translated strings when interpolation or pluralization applies.
- Must not use private string holder classes (`_BillingText`, etc.); add keys to `app_en.arb` and use `context.l10n` / `AppLocalizations`.
- Controllers and repositories must not own user-facing copy.

## Key naming

| Purpose | Example |
|---|---|
| Page title | `homeTitle` |
| Button | `commonSaveButton` |
| Field label | `profileFirstNameLabel` |
| Validation | `validationRequired` |
| Error | `networkTimeoutMessage` |

## Acceptance checklist

- `flutter gen-l10n` succeeds.
- No hard-coded user-facing strings in `lib/features`.
- Changing locale updates the app without touching business logic.

